Standard SHAP explainers require scalar function outputs ($f: \mathbb{R}^D \to \mathbb{R}$). Anomaly autoencoders, however, map $\mathbb{R}^{18} \to \mathbb{R}^{18}$. `backend/scripts/explain_autoencoder.py` introduces `AutoencoderMSEWrapper(nn.Module)`:
```python
class AutoencoderMSEWrapper(nn.Module):
    def forward(self, x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:
        recon = self.model(x)
        return ((x - recon) ** 2).mean(dim=-1, keepdim=True)
```
The wrapper outputs scalar per-sample MSE $(N, 1)$. `shap.GradientExplainer` uses 200 representative background samples drawn from benign transaction distributions to approximate Shapley values $\phi_i$ via integrated gradients: $\sum_{i=1}^{18} \phi_i \approx f(x) - E[f(x)]$. This quantifies exactly how much each tabular feature increased or decreased the transaction's anomaly score.

